#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153448 :tutorial/content "The **residue** of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the *unique* integer\n\n$$\n0 \\leq r < n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\na \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThis integer $r$ is sometimes called the **least residue** of $a$ modulo $n$.\n\nFor example, consider the following statements:\n\n$$\n25\\begin{vmatrix}≡1 & (mod6) \\\\ 25 & ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); 25\\begin{vmatrix}≡13 & (mod6) \\\\ 25 & ≡19\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ⋮\n$$\n\nAll of these statements are true. However, we say that \"the residue of $25$ modulo $6$ is ${\\color{blue}{1}}$\", because\n\n$$\n0\\leq {\\color{blue}{1}} < 6\n$$\n\nWe write this as\n\n$$\n25 \\textrm{mod} 6 = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that there are no parentheses around the $\\textrm{mod}$. When there are no parentheses, it means that we're treating $\\textrm{mod}$ as though it were a type of function. This function takes our integer $25$ as its input and returns the remainder that we get when $25$ is divided by $6$.\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n13mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n19mod6 &= 1 \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll present two methods of computing residues. Make sure that you understand *both* methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "25e74a5e-486f-54d0-816c-972f89285fc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9014 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153464 :tutorial/content "Some interesting problems in number theory require finding the last few digits of a large integer. We can use properties of congruences to help us solve these types of problems.\n\nConsider a positive number $a$, where the digit $d_1$ is in the ones place, $d_2$ is in the tens place, $d_3$ is in the hundreds place, and so on.\n\n$$\na = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= ⋯ + 100d_{3} + 10d_{2} + d_{1} \\\\\n&= 10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\na \\mid ≡10(⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 \\cdot (⋯ + 10d_{3} + d_{2}) + d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡0 + d_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡d_{1}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10$ returns the last digit of $a$.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n25\\,409\\,19{\\color{blue}3} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 10 = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nUsing similar reasoning,\n\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 100$ returns the last two digits of $a$, e.g.,\n$25\\,409\\,1{\\color{blue}93} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 100 = {\\color{blue}93}$.\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 1\\,000$ returns the last three digits of $a$, e.g.,\n$25\\,409\\,{\\color{blue}193} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 1\\,000 = {\\color{blue}193}$,\n\nand so on." :tutorial/id #uuid "399f1060-ce12-53e6-b522-98102a696b86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9041 :tutorial/title "Residues Modulo Powers of Ten"}]}
